.Dd November 27, 2026
.Dt PI0-PGP 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pi0-pgp
.Nd air-gapped system for private work on OpenPGP keys and USB storage
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The SD card of this Raspberry Pi Zero is read-only: whatever is not written to
a USB device or a security key is in memory, which never swaps, and is gone
when the Pi powers off.
.Ss Finding a USB device
USB sticks and drives are sda, sdb and so on, in the order the Pi finds them,
and mmcblk0 is the SD card, which is root's.
Their models and sizes, and the file systems they hold, tell them apart:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fdisk -l
blkid
.Ed
.Pp
The regular account can write to every USB stick and drive, so check the name
before any command here that erases one.
.Ss FAT file systems
systemd mounts a FAT file system labelled KEYS on
.Pa /mnt/keys ,
and one of the others, labelled or not, on
.Pa /mnt/usb-storage ,
when something reads the directory, and unmounts it after about 10 seconds of
disuse: wait 15 seconds before pulling the device out, partitioning it or making
a file system on it.
.Ss Partitioning
.Ic fdisk ,
.Ic sfdisk
and
.Ic cfdisk ,
which has a menu, write partition tables.
This erases /dev/sda, and gives it a partition for each of FAT, ext4 and BTRFS,
the last taking the rest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfdisk --wipe always --wipe-partitions always /dev/sda <<END
label: gpt
size=1GiB, type=EBD0A0A2-B9E5-4433-87C0-68B6B72699C7
size=1GiB, type=L
type=L
END
.Ed
.Pp
sfdisk and fdisk tell the regular account that re-reading the partition table
failed, which does no harm: udev has the kernel read it when they close the
device.
.Ss Making and checking file systems
These erase what each partition holds, and label its new file system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkfs.fat -F 32 -n PHOTOS /dev/sda1
mkfs.ext4 -L archive /dev/sda2
mkfs.btrfs -L backup /dev/sda3
.Ed
.Pp
Where there is a file system, mkfs.ext4 asks before it erases it, mkfs.btrfs
refuses to without
.Fl f ,
and mkfs.fat erases it without asking.
mkfs.btrfs warns the regular account that it could not register the device,
which does no harm: udev registers it.
.Pp
These check the file systems, which must not be mounted:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fsck.fat -n /dev/sda1
fsck.ext4 -f /dev/sda2
btrfs check /dev/sda3
.Ed
.Pp
fsck.ext4 asks before it repairs what it finds; the other two change nothing.
.Ss Moving files
The regular account reads and writes the FAT file systems that systemd mounts.
Only root can mount the rest, ext4 and BTRFS file systems among them.
Unmount each before you pull its device out.
As root, these copy a directory from a FAT stick to the file system on
/dev/sdb2:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount --mkdir -o nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sdb2 /tmp/archive
cp -r /mnt/usb-storage/photos /tmp/archive
umount /tmp/archive
.Ed
.Pp
The kernel reads what you plug in, and any file system that is mounted: after a
device that you do not trust, power off before you work on secrets.
.Ss Copying devices
.Ic dd
copies a device or a partition to another, or to a file, block by block.
This copies /dev/sda over /dev/sdb, which must be at least as large:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dd if=/dev/sda of=/dev/sdb bs=4M conv=fsync status=progress
.Ed
.Pp
dd overwrites what
.Cm of=
names without asking.
The kernel protects a mounted device or partition from it, but not a whole
device whose partitions are mounted: see that none is before you start.
Writing over flash storage does not erase it for certain, as it keeps blocks
that no write reaches.
.Ss Formatting a stick for the keys
This erases the stick, here /dev/sda:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkfs.fat -I -F 32 -n KEYS /dev/sda
.Ed
.Ss Making an OpenPGP key
For a security key such as a YubiKey, these steps make an OpenPGP key: a
primary key that only certifies, and subkeys to sign, to encrypt and to
authenticate.
None of them expires.
Log in as the regular account,
and put your own name and address in the commands.
For RSA keys, also give rsa4096 in place of ed25519 and of cv25519.
.Bl -enum -width 2n
.It
Plug in the YubiKey and a USB stick with a FAT file system labelled KEYS.
This lists what the stick holds, and fails without one:
.Bd -literal
ls /mnt/keys
.Ed
.It
Set the clock, because keys record when they are made, and the clock starts each
boot at the time the image was made:
.Bd -literal
timedatectl set-time 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS UTC'
.Ed
.It
Add the randomness of the YubiKey's own generator to the Pi's.
The first command makes GnuPG's home,
.Pa ~/.gnupg ,
which the second one needs:
.Bd -literal
gpg --list-keys
gpg-connect-agent 'SCD RANDOM 64' /bye | tee /dev/random | tail -n 1
gpgconf --kill gpg-agent
sleep 61
.Ed
.Pp
The second command prints OK if the YubiKey answered.
ERR means that none did: the keys then rest on the Pi's generator alone, and
there is nothing to wait for.
The kernel takes the randomness in within a minute, and the agent, which makes
the keys, has to start after that: hence the last two commands.
.It
Make the primary key.
gpg asks twice for a new passphrase, which protects the secret key and which the
next two steps ask for again.
gpg then prints the key's fingerprint, 40 hexadecimal digits:
.Bd -literal
gpg --quick-generate-key 'Your Name <you@example.org>' ed25519 cert 0
.Ed
.It
Add the subkeys to the key of that fingerprint:
.Bd -literal
fpr=FINGERPRINT
gpg --quick-add-key $fpr ed25519 sign 0
gpg --quick-add-key $fpr cv25519 encr 0
gpg --quick-add-key $fpr ed25519 auth 0
.Ed
.It
Write the secret key, the public key and the revocation certificate to the
stick:
.Bd -literal
backup=/mnt/keys/you@example.org
gpg -a --export-secret-keys $fpr > $backup-secret.asc
gpg -a --export $fpr > $backup-public.asc
cp ~/.gnupg/openpgp-revocs.d/$fpr.rev $backup-revoke.asc
sync
.Ed
.It
Have the agent forget the passphrase, which it keeps for ten minutes:
.Bd -literal
gpgconf --kill gpg-agent
.Ed
.El
.Ss Moving the subkeys to the YubiKey
Use
.Ic gpg\ --edit-key
and its
.Ic keytocard
command.
These show GnuPG's secret keys, the keys on the YubiKey, and the YubiKey's
model and applications:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpg --list-secret-keys
gpg --card-status
ykman info
.Ed
.Ss Several shells on the console
.Ic tmux
gives each shell a window or a pane of its own.
These start a session, come back to it, and end every session of the account:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tmux new-session -s work
tmux attach -t work
tmux kill-server
.Ed
.Pp
In a session, Ctrl-b % and Ctrl-b \(dq split the pane, Ctrl-b o goes to the
next one, and Ctrl-b d detaches from the session.
A session outlives a logout: whoever logs into the account next finds it, with
what its panes showed.
Power off before you leave the console to anybody else.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dd 1 ,
.Xr gpg 1 ,
.Xr gpg-connect-agent 1 ,
.Xr gpgconf 1 ,
.Xr timedatectl 1 ,
.Xr tmux 1 ,
.Xr ykman 1 ,
.Xr blkid 8 ,
.Xr btrfs 8 ,
.Xr btrfs-check 8 ,
.Xr cfdisk 8 ,
.Xr e2fsck 8 ,
.Xr fdisk 8 ,
.Xr fsck.fat 8 ,
.Xr mke2fs 8 ,
.Xr mkfs.btrfs 8 ,
.Xr mkfs.fat 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr sfdisk 8
